INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (1, 42, 'concept and commands', 'https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/dbms/what-is-object-relational-mapping-orm-in-dbms/<div><br></div><div>
<span style="color: white;">Prisma ORM Integration &amp; Schema Migration Guide</span><p style="color: white;">This report provides an overview of how Prisma is configured in the&nbsp;<strong>PrepTracker</strong>&nbsp;project and outlines the standard workflow for modifying the database schema (such as adding a new column) and exposing it through the API layer.</p><p style="color: white;"><br></p><span style="color: white;"></span><span style="color: white;">1. How Prisma Works in PrepTracker</span><p style="color: white;">Prisma is utilized as the Object-Relational Mapper (ORM) in the&nbsp;server&nbsp;directory. It maps TypeScript objects to tables in a PostgreSQL database.</p><span style="color: white;">Key Components:</span><ol style="color: white;"><li><strong>Schema File (schema.prisma):</strong>&nbsp;Defines the database connection parameters (datasource), the generated client settings, and the database models/enums.</li><li><strong>Migrations Directory (migrations/):</strong>&nbsp;Contains SQL files representing historical changes applied to the database. Each migration is recorded in the&nbsp;_prisma_migrations&nbsp;table to track which changes have run.</li><li><strong>Prisma Client (@prisma/client):</strong>&nbsp;A type-safe database client auto-generated based on&nbsp;schema.prisma. It is imported in routes like&nbsp;server/src/routes/topics.ts&nbsp;to query the database.</li><li><strong>Seed Script (seed.ts):</strong>&nbsp;Populates the database with initial development data.</li><li><br></li></ol><span style="color: white;"></span><span style="color: white;">2. Step-by-Step Guide: Adding a Column to a Table</span><p style="color: white;">To add a new column to a table (e.g., adding&nbsp;description&nbsp;to the&nbsp;Topic&nbsp;table) and have it fully functional end-to-end, follow these steps:</p><p style="color: white;"><br></p><span style="color: white;">Step A: Update the Prisma Schema</span><p style="color: white;">Open&nbsp;schema.prisma&nbsp;and locate the model you want to change. Add the new field definition:</p><span style="color: white;"><div><div><div>prisma</div><div></div></div><div><div><div><div><div>model Topic {</div></div><div><div>  id             Int           @id @default(autoincrement())</div></div><div><div>  category       String</div></div><div><div>  name           String</div></div><div><div>  description    String?       @db.Text // &lt;-- NEW COLUMN ADDED HERE (nullable Text field)</div></div><div><div>  masteryLevel   MasteryLevel  @default(NOT_STARTED)</div></div><div><div>  lastPracticed  DateTime?</div></div><div><div>  notes          String?       @db.Text</div></div><div><div>  topicNotes     TopicNote[]</div></div><div><div>}</div></div></div></div></div></div><div><br></div></span><span style="color: white;"></span><span style="color: white;">Step B: Run Migration &amp; Regenerate Client</span><p style="color: white;">Open your terminal in the&nbsp;server/&nbsp;directory and run:</p><span style="color: white;"><div><div><div>bash</div><div></div></div><div><div><div><div><div>npx prisma migrate dev --name add_description_to_topic</div></div></div></div></div></div></span><div style="color: white;"><p>NOTE</p><p>This single command automatically:</p><ol><li>Compares your Prisma schema with the database state.</li><li>Generates a new SQL migration file inside the&nbsp;prisma/migrations&nbsp;directory.</li><li>Executes the SQL migrations on your local database.</li><li>Regenerates the&nbsp;@prisma/client&nbsp;types inside&nbsp;node_modules&nbsp;so autocomplete and type checking work instantly.</li></ol></div><span style="color: white;"></span><span style="color: white;">Step C: Expose the Column in the API (Backend)</span><p style="color: white;">Update the Express routes to handle the new column. For example, in&nbsp;server/src/routes/topics.ts:</p><ol style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>POST route (Creation):</strong>&nbsp;Update&nbsp;/api/topics&nbsp;to parse and insert the new field:</p><div><div><div>typescript</div><div></div></div><div><div><div><div><div>const { category, name, description } = req.body;</div></div><div><div>const topic = await prisma.topic.create({</div></div><div><div>  data: {</div></div><div><div>    category,</div></div><div><div>    name,</div></div><div><div>    description, // &lt;-- Save description</div></div><div><div>    masteryLevel: MasteryLevel.NOT_STARTED</div></div><div><div>  }</div></div><div><div>});</div></div></div></div></div></div></li><li><p><strong>PUT route (Update):</strong>&nbsp;Update&nbsp;/api/topics/:id&nbsp;to allow updating the field:</p><div><div><div>typescript</div><div></div></div><div><div><div><div><div>const { name, masteryLevel, notes, lastPracticed, description } = req.body;</div></div><div><div>const updateData: any = {};</div></div><div><div>// ...</div></div><div><div>if (description !== undefined) updateData.description = description;</div></div></div></div></div></div></li><li><div><br></div></li></ol><span style="color: white;"></span><span style="color: white;">Step D: Update Frontend TypeScript Types</span><p style="color: white;">Open&nbsp;client/src/types/index.ts&nbsp;and add the new field to the interface so the compiler recognizes it:</p><span style="color: white;"><div><div><div>typescript</div><div></div></div><div><div><div><div><div>export interface Topic {</div></div><div><div>  id: number;</div></div><div><div>  category: Category;</div></div><div><div>  name: string;</div></div><div><div>  description: string | null; // &lt;-- Add description here</div></div><div><div>  masteryLevel: MasteryLevel;</div></div><div><div>  lastPracticed: string | null;</div></div><div><div>  notes: string | null;</div></div><div><div>  topicNotes: TopicNote[];</div></div><div><div>}</div></div></div></div></div></div></span><span style="color: white;"></span><span style="color: white;">Step E: Update Frontend Components</span><p style="color: white;">Use the new field inside&nbsp;client/src/pages/Topics.tsx&nbsp;(e.g., rendering the description text, or including it in forms when calling the mutations).</p><div><span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;">Important Commands</span></div><span style="color: white;">1. npx prisma migrate dev-&gt;Generates a new SQL migration, runs it, and regenerates the Prisma client. Whenever you modify&nbsp;schema.prisma&nbsp;in development.</span></div><div>2. <span style="color: white;">npx prisma generate-&gt;Regenerates&nbsp;@prisma/client&nbsp;without creating a database migration. If you cloned the repo fresh, updated dependencies, or manually modified schema definitions without migrating.</span></div><div>3. <span style="color: white;">npx prisma db push-&gt;Pushes the schema directly to the database without creating migration tracking files.Useful for rapid prototyping/testing on a temporary database (not recommended for production/shared DBs).</span></div><div>4. <span style="color: white;">npx prisma studio-&gt;Starts a visual database inspector GUI in your browser at&nbsp;http://localhost:5555.When you want to inspect, add, or edit database rows quickly without writing SQL queries.</span></div><div>5. <span style="color: white;">npx prisma db seed-&gt;Executes the seed script defined in&nbsp;package.json&nbsp;to populate local tables.To populate default baseline categories or tasks.</span>
6. <span style="color: white;">npx prisma migrate status-&gt;</span><span style="color: white;">Checks if there are any unapplied migrations or synchronization issues.</span><span style="color: white;">If you suspect the local database has desynced from the code</span></div>
<div>










</div>', '2026-07-18 08:36:03.761', '2026-07-18 08:53:06.72');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (11, 46, 'Note 1', 'Main source: https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/dns/what-is-dns/<div>
<div style="color: white;"><div><div><div><p>The Domain Name System (DNS) is the phonebook of the Internet. Humans access information online through&nbsp;domain names, like nytimes.com or espn.com. Web browsers interact through&nbsp;Internet Protocol (IP)&nbsp;addresses. DNS translates domain names to&nbsp;IP addresses&nbsp;so browsers can load Internet resources.</p></div></div></div></div><p style="color: white;">Each device connected to the Internet has a unique IP address which other machines use to find the device. DNS servers eliminate the need for humans to memorize IP addresses such as 192.168.1.1 (in IPv4), or more complex newer alphanumeric IP addresses such as 2400:cb00:2048:1::c629:d7a2 (in IPv6).</p>

<span style="color: white;">How does DNS work?</span><p style="color: white;">The process of DNS resolution involves converting a hostname (such as www.example.com) into a computer-friendly IP address (such as 192.168.1.1). An IP address is given to each device on the Internet, and that address is necessary to find the appropriate Internet device - like a street address is used to find a particular home. When a user wants to load a webpage, a translation must occur between what a user types into their web browser (example.com) and the machine-friendly address necessary to locate the example.com webpage.</p>

<span style="color: white; font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;">The 4 DNS Servers: How Your Browser Finds a Website</span><p style="color: white;"><br></p><p style="color: white;">When you type a URL (like www.amazon.com) into your browser, your computer doesn''t actually know where that is. Computers communicate using IP addresses (like 192.0.2.44), not human words.</p><p style="color: white;">To translate www.amazon.com into an IP address, your request travels through four specific DNS (Domain Name System) servers.</p><span style="color: white;">The Real-World Analogy: Finding a Specific Employee</span><p style="color: white;">Imagine you are trying to find the phone number for "John Doe in the HR Department at Amazon."</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>1. The DNS Recursor (The Personal Assistant)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>What it is:</strong> This is a server usually operated by your Internet Service Provider (ISP) or a public provider like Google (8.8.8.8).</p></li><li><p><strong>Its Job:</strong> It acts as your personal assistant. Your browser hands the Recursor the URL and says, <em>"Go find the IP address for this, I''ll wait here."</em> The Recursor is responsible for doing all the legwork of talking to the other three servers.</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>2. The Root Nameserver (The Building Front Desk)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>What it is:</strong> There are 13 logical Root Nameserver addresses globally. They are the top of the internet''s hierarchy.</p></li><li><p><strong>Its Job:</strong> It doesn''t know the exact IP address, but it knows which server manages the <strong>extension</strong> of your URL (like .com, .org, or .net).</p></li><li><p><em>Analogy:</em> Your assistant walks into a massive skyscraper and asks the front desk, <em>"Where is Amazon?"</em> The front desk says, <em>"I don''t know the exact desk, but all commercial businesses (.com) are on the 5th floor. Go ask the receptionist there."</em></p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>3. The TLD (Top-Level Domain) Nameserver (The Floor Receptionist)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>What it is:</strong> This server maintains information for all the domain names that share a common extension (the "Top-Level Domain"). There is a TLD server for .com, a different one for .org, etc.</p></li><li><p><strong>Its Job:</strong> It knows exactly which specific company''s server manages the domain you are looking for (e.g., amazon.com).</p></li><li><p><em>Analogy:</em> Your assistant goes to the 5th floor (.com) and asks the floor receptionist for Amazon. The receptionist says, <em>"Amazon''s specific HR office is in Room 502. Go ask the manager in there."</em></p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>4. The Authoritative Nameserver (The Department Manager / Final Source of Truth)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>What it is:</strong> This is the final server in the chain. It is actually owned or configured by the company hosting the website (like Amazon or their cloud provider).</p></li><li><p><strong>Its Job:</strong> It holds the exact DNS records (A Records) for the specific subdomain (like www). It holds the actual IP address.</p></li><li><p><em>Analogy:</em> Your assistant walks into Room 502 (amazon.com), asks for www, and the manager hands them John Doe''s exact phone number: 192.0.2.44.</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>The Complete Flow (Example: www.amazon.com)</span><ol style="color: white;"><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Browser:</strong> "Hey <strong>Recursor</strong>, what is the IP for www.amazon.com?"</p></li><li><p><strong>Recursor -&gt; Root Server:</strong> "Where can I find .com?"</p><ul><li><p><em>Root Server replies:</em> "Here is the IP for the .com <strong>TLD Server</strong>."</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Recursor -&gt; TLD Server:</strong> "Where can I find amazon.com?"</p><ul><li><p><em>TLD Server replies:</em> "Here is the IP for Amazon''s <strong>Authoritative Server</strong>."</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Recursor -&gt; Authoritative Server:</strong> "What is the exact IP for www.amazon.com?"</p><ul><li><p><em>Authoritative Server replies:</em> "The IP address is 205.251.242.103."</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Recursor -&gt; Browser:</strong> "I found it! The IP is 205.251.242.103."</p></li><li><p><em>Your browser instantly connects to that IP, and the web page loads.</em></p></li></ol><p style="color: white;"><em>(Note: To make this process lightning fast, the DNS Recursor and your browser will "cache" or memorize this IP address so they don''t have to ask the other three servers again the next time you visit).</em></p>



</div>', '2026-07-20 11:14:56.057', '2026-07-22 06:38:51.56');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (3, 43, 'FAilover patterns', '
<p style="color: white;">Failover is an availability pattern that is used to ensure that a system can continue to function in the event of a failure. It involves having a backup component or system that can take over in the event of a failure.</p><p style="color: white;">In a failover system, there is a primary component that is responsible for handling requests, and a secondary (or backup) component that is on standby. The primary component is monitored for failures, and if it fails, the secondary component is activated to take over its duties. This allows the system to continue functioning with minimal disruption.</p><p style="color: white;">Failover can be implemented in various ways, such as active-passive, active-active, and hot-standby.</p><p style="color: white;">
Disadvantages of Failover</p><ul style="color: white;"><li>Fail-over adds more hardware and additional complexity.</li><li>There is a potential for loss of data if the active system fails before any newly written data can be replicated to the passive.</li></ul><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"><br></span></div><div>
<span style="color: white;">1. Active-Passive (The "Backup" Strategy)</span><p style="color: white;">In this setup, Server A is doing all the work, and Server B is just sitting there waiting for Server A to die. Because only one server is taking traffic, making sure data is consistent is much easier.</p><p style="color: white;">The text mentions two ways to handle the data:</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Database Replication / File Sync:</strong> Server A writes data to its own hard drive, and then immediately copies (replicates) that data to Server B''s hard drive. (Like doing your homework and letting your friend copy it).</p></li><li><p><strong>Shared Storage:</strong> Instead of having two separate hard drives, both Server A and Server B are plugged into one massive external hard drive (a shared repository like an AWS EBS volume or a NAS). Since there is only <em>one</em> hard drive, there is nothing to sync! Server A writes to it. If Server A crashes, Server B wakes up, looks at the exact same hard drive, and picks up right where A left off.</p></li><li><p><br></p></li></ul><span style="color: white;">2. Active-Active (The "Load Balancing" Strategy)</span><p style="color: white;">In this setup, Server A and Server B are <em>both</em> working and taking user traffic at the exact same time. This is much harder to keep consistent.</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Real-time data replication:</strong> If a user updates their profile on Server A, Server A must instantly send that update to Server B, otherwise Server B will serve old data to the next user.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>The "Session" Problem:</strong> Imagine you log into an e-commerce site, and Server A handles your login. Server A saves a note in its RAM: <em>"User123 is logged in."</em> But what if you click "Add to Cart" and the load balancer sends that request to Server B? Server B checks its RAM, doesn''t see your login, and kicks you out!</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>The Solution (Stateless or External Store):</strong> To fix this, you must extract the "memory" out of the individual servers.</p><ul><li><p><em>Stateless:</em> The server remembers nothing. Instead, the user''s browser sends a secure token (like a JWT) with every single click to prove they are logged in.</p></li><li><p><em>External Session Store:</em> Both Server A and Server B connect to a shared, super-fast memory cache (like <strong>Redis</strong>). When you log in, Server A writes "User123 is logged in" into Redis. When you click "Add to Cart" and go to Server B, Server B checks Redis, sees you are logged in, and allows it.</p></li></ul></li></ul>
</div><div><br></div><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">https://dev.to/decoders_lord/system-design-availability-patterns-104i</span></div><p style="color: white;">

</p>

', '2026-07-18 09:06:44.8', '2026-07-18 09:16:07.08');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (2, 41, 'Main points', '
<span style="color: white;">25 System Design Fundamentals</span><ol style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Domain Name System (DNS)</strong>&nbsp;– Translates human-friendly website names (like google.com) into IP addresses computers use.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Load Balancer</strong>&nbsp;– Distributes incoming traffic across multiple servers to keep the system fast and reliable.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>API Gateway</strong>&nbsp;– A single entry point that routes client requests to backend services in a microservices system.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>CDN (Content Delivery Network)</strong>&nbsp;– A network of servers that delivers static content (like images or videos) from locations closer to the user.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Forward Proxy vs Reverse Proxy</strong>&nbsp;– A forward proxy hides the client from the server; a reverse proxy hides the server from the client.</p></li><li><p>https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/difference-between-forward-proxy-and-reverse-proxy/</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Caching</strong>&nbsp;– Temporarily stores frequently used data to speed up future access.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Data Partitioning</strong>&nbsp;– Splits large datasets into smaller chunks (shards) to spread the load across servers.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Database Replication</strong>&nbsp;– Creates and maintains copies of data across different servers for better availability and fault tolerance.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Distributed Messaging Systems</strong>&nbsp;– Enable services to communicate by sending messages asynchronously via a queue (e.g. Kafka, RabbitMQ).</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Microservices</strong>&nbsp;– A system design where different features are split into small, independent services that work together.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>NoSQL Database</strong>&nbsp;– A flexible, schema-less database designed for fast storage and retrieval of large amounts of data (e.g. MongoDB).</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Database Index</strong>&nbsp;– Like a table of contents for a database that helps find data faster.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Distributed File Systems</strong>&nbsp;– A system that lets you store and access files across many servers as if they were on one machine.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Notification System</strong>&nbsp;– Sends alerts or updates to users through email, push notifications, or SMS based on events.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Full-Text Search</strong>&nbsp;– Lets you search for words or phrases in documents or data like Google search does.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Distributed Coordination Services</strong>&nbsp;– Tools (like ZooKeeper) that help manage and synchronize tasks across distributed systems.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Heartbeat</strong>&nbsp;– A small, regular signal sent between components to check if they’re still alive and responsive.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Checksum</strong>&nbsp;– A small piece of data used to detect errors in files or messages.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Latency vs Throughput</strong>&nbsp;– Latency is the time it takes to process one request; throughput is how many requests are handled per second.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Availability vs Reliability</strong>&nbsp;– Availability means the system is up and running; reliability means it works correctly over time without failure.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>CAP Theorem</strong>&nbsp;– In a distributed system, you can only guarantee two out of three: consistency, availability, and partition tolerance.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Consistency Patterns</strong>&nbsp;– Strategies for making sure all users see the same data, either instantly or eventually.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Scalability</strong>&nbsp;– The ability of a system to handle increasing traffic, data, or users without breaking or slowing down.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Long Polling vs WebSockets</strong>&nbsp;– Long polling repeatedly asks the server for updates; WebSockets keep a live connection for real-time communication.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Rate Limiting</strong>&nbsp;– Controls how many requests a user or client can make in a certain time to protect the system from abuse.</p></li></ol><div><br></div>

', '2026-07-18 08:57:49.57', '2026-07-18 09:16:53.529');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (4, 43, 'Replication patterns', '
<p style="color: white;">Replication is an availability pattern that involves having multiple copies of the same data stored in different locations. In the event of a failure, the data can be retrieved from a different location. There are two main types of replication: Master-Master replication and Master-Slave replication.</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Master-Master replication:</strong>&nbsp;In this type of replication, multiple servers are configured as "masters," and each one can accept read and write operations. This allows for high availability and allows any of the servers to take over if one of them fails. However, this type of replication can lead to conflicts if multiple servers update the same data at the same time, so some conflict resolution mechanism is needed to handle this.</p></li><li><p><strong>Master-Slave replication:</strong>&nbsp;In this type of replication, one server is designated as the "master" and handles all write operations, while multiple "slave" servers handle read operations. If the master fails, one of the slaves can be promoted to take its place. This type of replication is simpler to set up and maintain compared to Master-Master replication.</p></li></ul>
https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/replication-in-system-design/', '2026-07-18 09:19:04.614', '2026-07-18 09:22:53.79');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (8, 45, 'Note 1', '<span style="color: white;">Background Jobs (Asynchronous Processing)</span><p style="color: white;">In System Design, a <strong>Background Job</strong> is any piece of code that runs "behind the scenes" instead of making the user wait for it to finish.</p><p style="color: white;">To understand why they are critical, you have to understand the difference between Synchronous and Asynchronous processing.</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>The Problem: Synchronous Blocking</span><p style="color: white;">Imagine you are building an app like Instagram. A user uploads a 500MB 4K video.
-If your API processes this <strong>synchronously</strong>:</p><ol style="color: white;"><li><p>-User clicks "Upload".</p></li><li><p>-The server receives the file and starts compressing it, generating thumbnails, and saving it to a database.</p></li><li><p>-This takes 45 seconds.</p></li><li><p>-For 45 seconds, the user''s app is frozen with a loading spinner. If their internet drops at second 44, the whole thing fails.</p></li></ol><p style="color: white;">-This is terrible UX (User Experience) and terrible system design. The API server should be free to handle new requests, not stuck compressing a video.</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>The Solution: Asynchronous Background Jobs</span><p style="color: white;">Instead of making the user wait, we decouple the heavy work from the main API.</p><ol style="color: white;"><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>1.The Producer (Your API):</strong> The user clicks "Upload". The API receives the video, saves the raw file to a bucket (like AWS S3), and instantly replies to the user: <em>"HTTP 202 Accepted: Your video is processing and will be live soon!"</em></p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>2.The Queue (The Middleman):</strong> The API creates a small message (e.g., {"task": "compress_video", "video_id": 123}) and drops it into a <strong>Message Queue</strong>.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>3.The Consumer (The Worker):</strong> A completely separate server (a Background Worker) is constantly watching the queue. It sees the message, pulls the raw video from S3, compresses it, and updates the database when finished.</p></li></ol><p style="color: white;">Because the Worker is doing the heavy lifting, the API is instantly free to handle the next user.</p><span style="color: white;">Standard Architecture &amp; Tools</span><p style="color: white;"><br></p><p style="color: white;"><br></p><p style="color: white;">To build this, you need three components:</p><ol style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>1.Message Broker / Queue:</strong> The place that holds the messages.</p><ul><li><p><em>Common Tools:</em> <strong>Redis</strong> (using Lists/PubSub), <strong>RabbitMQ</strong> (standard message queue), <strong>Apache Kafka</strong> (high-throughput event streaming), <strong>AWS SQS</strong>.</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>2.Task Framework:</strong> The software that manages the workers, pulls from the queue, and executes the code.</p><ul><li><p><em>Common Tools:</em> <strong>Celery</strong> (Python), <strong>Sidekiq</strong> (Ruby), <strong>BullMQ</strong> (Node.js/TypeScript).</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>3.Worker Nodes:</strong> These are just standard servers (often running as <strong>Docker containers</strong> managed by <strong>Kubernetes</strong>) whose only job is to execute the functions defined by the Task Framework.</p></li></ol><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Common Real-World Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Sending Emails/SMS:</strong> You never send an email synchronously during a user signup. You queue it so the signup API returns instantly.</p></li><li><p><strong>Generating Reports:</strong> A user asks for a CSV export of their last 5 years of transactions.</p></li><li><p><strong>Payment Processing:</strong> Communicating with Stripe or bank APIs, which might be slow.</p></li><li><p><strong>Scheduled Tasks (Cron Jobs):</strong> "Run this job every night at midnight to calculate daily revenue."</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;">----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></div><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>🚨 Interview "Gotchas" (How to impress the interviewer)</span><p style="color: white;">If you just say "I''ll put it in a queue," the interviewer will immediately ask you these follow-up questions to test if you actually understand distributed systems:</p><p style="color: white;"><strong>1. "What happens if the worker crashes halfway through processing the job?"</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>The Answer:</em> The queue must require an <strong>ACK (Acknowledgement)</strong>. The worker pulls the message but the queue <em>hides</em> it rather than deleting it. If the worker finishes successfully, it sends an ACK, and the queue deletes the message. If the worker crashes, it never sends the ACK, a timeout expires, and the queue makes the message visible again for a different worker to pick up.</p></li></ul><p style="color: white;"><strong>2. "What if the job keeps failing forever? Will it clog the queue?"</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>The Answer:</em> You implement a <strong>Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)</strong>. You configure the system to retry a failed job a set number of times (e.g., 3 times). If it fails 3 times, the broker automatically moves the message out of the main queue and into the DLQ. Engineers can then inspect the DLQ later to figure out why those specific jobs are failing without slowing down the rest of the system.</p></li></ul><p style="color: white;"><strong>3. "Since jobs might be retried, how do you handle a scenario where a user gets charged twice?"</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>The Answer:</em> Background jobs must be <strong>Idempotent</strong>. This is a massive buzzword. Idempotency means that no matter how many times you run the exact same job, the end result is the same as if it ran only once. Before charging the card, the worker checks the database: <em>"Have I already processed transaction ID 123?"</em> If yes, it skips it.</p></li></ul>

', '2026-07-19 17:09:57.801', '2026-07-23 07:11:21.707');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (9, 45, 'BJ Flow', '
<span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;"><span style="color: white;">                                                                                           Technical Flow: Asynchronous File Processing</span><span style="color: white;">The Scenario</span></span><p style="color: white;"><br></p><p style="color: white;">A user wants to upload a 1GB raw video to your web app. The app needs to compress this video, generate a thumbnail, and make it available for streaming.</p><span style="color: white;">The Infrastructure (Dockerized):</span><span style="color: white;">Imagine this system running as four separate Docker containers on your server:</span><ol style="color: white;"><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>1.API Container:</strong> Runs your Python backend (Django/FastAPI).</p></li><li><p><strong>2.Database Container:</strong> Runs PostgreSQL (stores user and video metadata).</p></li><li><p><strong>3.Redis Container:</strong> Runs in-memory data structures (acts as our Message Broker).</p></li><li><p><strong>4.Celery Worker Container:</strong> Runs the Celery daemon (waits for tasks and executes them).</p></li></ol><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>The Step-by-Step Execution Flow</span><span style="color: white;">Phase 1: The Fast API Response (Synchronous)</span><p style="color: white;"><strong>1. The Upload:</strong>
To prevent your API server from choking on a massive 1GB file, the frontend app requests a "Presigned URL" from your API, and then uploads the 1GB file <em>directly</em> to cloud storage (like AWS S3).</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>2. The Trigger:</strong>
Once the upload to S3 finishes, the frontend sends a tiny, fast JSON request to your API:
POST /process-video { "file_path": "s3://raw-videos/user123_vid.mp4" }</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>3. Database Record Created:</strong>
Your API receives this request and inserts a new row into the PostgreSQL database:
[ID: 99, User: ''user123'', Status: ''PENDING'', URL: null]</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>4. The Handoff to Celery:</strong>
In your API code, you call the Celery task function. It looks something like this:
compress_video_task.delay(video_id=99)
Behind the scenes, the Celery library takes this function call, turns it into a JSON message {"task": "compress_video", "args": [99]}, and pushes it into the <strong>Redis</strong> container.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>5. The Instant Reply:</strong>
The API immediately responds to the frontend: HTTP 202 Accepted: {"status": "processing", "video_id": 99}.
<em>Time elapsed for the user: 50 milliseconds. The user''s app is perfectly responsive.</em></p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><span style="font-weight: bold;">Phase 2: The Heavy Lifting (Asynchronous)</span></span><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>6. The Worker Wakes Up:</strong>
The <strong>Celery Worker Container</strong> is constantly listening to the Redis queue. It sees the new message pop into Redis, grabs it, and acknowledges receipt.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>7. State Update (In Progress):</strong>
The Celery Worker connects to PostgreSQL and updates the video row: Status: ''PROCESSING''.
<em>(If the user refreshes their webpage right now, the frontend checks the DB and shows them a "Processing..." spinner).</em></p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>8. The CPU-Intensive Work:</strong>
The Celery Worker securely downloads the 1GB file from S3 into its own container memory. It runs FFmpeg (a video processing tool) to compress the video down to 100MB and grabs a screenshot for the thumbnail.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>9. Uploading the Result:</strong>
The Worker uploads the newly compressed 100MB video and the thumbnail back up to AWS S3.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>10. Final State Update:</strong>
The Worker connects to PostgreSQL one last time:
[ID: 99, Status: ''COMPLETED'', URL: ''s3://processed-videos/final.mp4'']</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>11. The Notification (Optional):</strong>
Before going back to sleep, the Celery worker triggers a WebSocket event or a push notification that goes to the frontend: <em>"Ping! Your video is ready!"</em></p><span style="color: white;">What happens if the Celery Worker crashes at Step 8?</span><p style="color: white;">Because Celery is designed for distributed systems, it uses <strong>Acknowledgements (ACKs)</strong>.
When the worker grabbed the message at Step 6, Redis didn''t delete it; it just hid it. If the Celery container runs out of RAM and crashes at Step 8, it never sends the "Task Finished" ACK back to Redis.
After a timeout, Redis realizes the worker died, un-hides the message, and hands it to a different Celery worker to try again!</p>

', '2026-07-19 18:37:28.197', '2026-07-23 07:18:54.297');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (14, 46, 'Note 2', '<span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;">
</span><div style="color: white;"><span style="font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;">DNS records</span> are instructions stored in authoritative servers that translate human-readable domain names (e.g., example.com) into machine-readable IP addresses. They act as the internet''s phonebook, ensuring that when you type a web address or send an email, your request is routed to the correct destination. [1, 2, 3]</div><div style="color: white;"></div><div style="color: white;"><br></div><div style="color: white;">Essential DNS Record Types</div><div style="color: white;"><div>Different DNS records handle different internet functions:</div></div><ul style="color: white;"><div><li><strong>A Record (Address):</strong> Maps your domain name to an IPv4 address. For example, pointing example.com to 192.0.2.1. [1, 2, 3, 4]</li></div><div><li><strong>AAAA Record (Quad-A):</strong> Maps your domain name to an IPv6 address. [1]</li></div><div><li><strong>CNAME Record (Canonical Name):</strong> Creates an alias that points one domain or subdomain to another domain name rather than an IP address. [1, 2]</li></div><div><li><strong>MX Record (Mail Exchange):</strong> Directs incoming emails to the correct mail servers responsible for your domain. [1]</li></div><div><li><strong>TXT Record (Text):</strong> Allows you to store arbitrary text in your DNS. It is heavily used for security and verification, like SPF, DKIM, and DMARC configurations. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</li></div><div><li><strong>NS Record (Name Server):</strong> Specifies which authoritative name servers are responsible for your domain''s DNS zone. [1, 2]</li></div><div><li><strong>SOA Record (Start of Authority):</strong> Contains critical administrative information about a DNS zone, including the primary name server and the email address of the administrator. [1, 2]</li></div></ul><span style="color: white;"><div></div><div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Record Structure</div></div><div><div>Every DNS record contains a few key components:</div></div><ul><div><li><strong>Name / Host:</strong> The specific subdomain or root domain (e.g., www or @ for the root).</li></div><div><li><strong>Type:</strong> The type of record (A, MX, TXT, etc.).</li></div><div><li><strong>Value / Data:</strong> The destination, such as an IP address or hostname.</li></div><div><li><strong>TTL (Time to Live):</strong> A value measured in seconds that tells recursive servers how long to cache the record before checking for updates</li><li><br></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li>
The Big Difference: Recursive vs. Authoritative<p>Before diving into the 4 steps, it helps to understand the two main categories of DNS servers:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Recursive DNS (The Middleman / The Assistant):</strong> This server doesn''t actually own any official website records. Its only job is to do the searching for you. It asks other servers questions, caches (memorizes) the answers to speed things up later, and reports back to your computer.</p></li><li><p><strong>Authoritative DNS (The Boss / The Source of Truth):</strong> This server doesn''t search for anything. It is the official owner of the domain''s DNS records. It waits for Recursive servers to ask it questions, and it provides the final, official IP address.</p></li></ul>

1. The Definitions (Noun vs. Verb)<ul><li><p><strong>The Recursive Resolver (The Machine):</strong> This is the actual physical server (usually owned by your ISP or Google). Its entire job is to act as a middleman.</p></li><li><p><strong>A Recursive Query (The Question):</strong> This is a specific <em>type of request</em>. When a machine sends a "Recursive Query," it is saying: <em>"I want the absolute final answer. Do not give me a referral. I will wait right here until you do all the searching and bring me the final IP address."</em></p></li></ul><p><em>(Note: The opposite of a Recursive Query is an <strong>Iterative Query</strong>. An Iterative Query means: "Give me the final answer, OR just tell me who I should ask next.")</em></p><li><br></li>2. The Serial Flow (How they fit together)<p>Here is exactly how the machine and the queries interact in chronological order when you type amazon.com into your browser.</p><p><strong>Step 1: The Browser''s Demand</strong>
Your laptop doesn''t want to do any hard work. So, your web browser sends a <strong>Recursive Query</strong> to the <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong>.</p><ul><li><p><em>Translation:</em> The browser says, "Hey Resolver, get me the IP for amazon.com. I''m going to wait right here. Don''t come back until you have it."</p></li></ul><p><strong>Step 2: The Resolver Accepts the Job</strong>
The <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong> accepts this demanding request. Because it accepted a <em>Recursive Query</em>, the burden of doing all the legwork now falls entirely on this server.</p><p><strong>Step 3: The Resolver Starts Searching (Using Iterative Queries)</strong>
The Recursive Resolver now has to talk to the rest of the internet (the Root, TLD, and Authoritative servers) to find the answer.
However, it does <strong>not</strong> send Recursive Queries to them. The big internet servers are too busy to do the searching for you. So, the Resolver sends them <strong>Iterative Queries</strong>.</p><ul><li><p><em>Translation:</em> The Resolver asks the Root Server: "Do you know amazon.com? If not, just tell me who to ask next."</p></li><li><p><em>Root Server replies:</em> "I don''t know, but go ask the .com TLD Server."</p></li></ul><p><strong>Step 4: The Resolver Follows the Breadcrumbs</strong>
The <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong> sends another <strong>Iterative Query</strong>, this time to the TLD Server.</p><ul><li><p><em>Translation:</em> The Resolver asks the TLD Server: "Do you know amazon.com?"</p></li><li><p><em>TLD Server replies:</em> "I don''t know, but go ask Amazon''s Authoritative Server."</p></li></ul><p><strong>Step 5: The Final Answer</strong>
The <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong> sends one last <strong>Iterative Query</strong> to the Authoritative Server, gets the final IP address, and brings it all the way back to your browser.</p>The Summary<p>If you want to remember it perfectly for an interview, just remember this sequence:</p><ol><li><p>Your Browser sends a <strong>Recursive Query</strong> to the <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong>.</p></li><li><p>The <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong> then sends multiple <strong>Iterative Queries</strong> to the rest of the internet to hunt down the answer for you.</p></li></ol><div><br></div>


</li><li><div>-----------------------------Better version--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</div></li><li><div>
The 3 Types of DNS Queries<p>To understand how DNS queries work, we have to look at the <strong>"Body Parts"</strong> (the machines) and the <strong>"Questions"</strong> (the queries).</p>The Body Parts (The Machines)<ol><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><span style="font-style: italic;"><strong>The Client (Stub Resolver):</strong> Your laptop, phone, or web browser. It is lazy and doesn''t want to do any heavy lifting.</span></p></li><li><p><span style="font-style: italic;"><strong>The Recursive Resolver:</strong> Your ISP or Google (8.8.8.8). The hard-working middleman.</span></p></li><li><p><span style="font-style: italic;"><strong>The Big Internet Servers:</strong> The Root, TLD, and Authoritative servers.</span></p></li></ol><div><br></div>1. The Recursive Query (The "Lazy Demand")<ul><li><p><strong>Who makes it?</strong> The <strong>Client</strong> sends this to the <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong>.</p></li><li><p><strong>What does it mean?</strong> "I want the absolute final IP address. I will not accept a referral. I am going to wait right here until you go find it and bring it back to me. If you can''t find it, give me an error."</p></li><li><p><strong>Real-World Analogy:</strong> A CEO tells their assistant: <em>"Get me John Doe on the phone. I don''t care how you do it, just don''t come back into my office until he is on the line."</em></p></li></ul><div><br></div>2. The Iterative Query (The "Detective Work")<ul><li><p><strong>Who makes it?</strong> The <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong> sends these to the <strong>Root</strong>, <strong>TLD</strong>, and <strong>Authoritative</strong> servers.</p></li><li><p><strong>What does it mean?</strong> "Give me the final IP address, OR just give me a referral to the next server I should talk to. I will do the walking."</p></li><li><p><strong>Why?</strong> The big internet servers are too busy to do recursive searches for millions of people. They refuse Recursive Queries. They only accept Iterative Queries so they can quickly say, <em>"I don''t know, go ask that guy,"</em> and move on to the next request.</p></li><li><p><strong>Real-World Analogy:</strong> The assistant walks into a building and asks the front desk: <em>"Where is John Doe?"</em> The front desk replies: <em>"I don''t know exactly, but he is in the HR department on the 5th floor. Go ask them."</em></p></li></ul><div><br></div>3. The Non-Recursive Query (The "Instant Answer")<ul><li><p><strong>Who makes it?</strong></p><ul><li><p>Scenario A: The <strong>Client</strong> asks the <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong>.</p></li><li><p>Scenario B: The <strong>Recursive Resolver</strong> asks the <strong>Authoritative Server</strong>.</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>What does it mean?</strong> "Give me the IP address immediately. You do not need to go search for it, because I know you already have it."</p></li><li><p><strong>When does this happen?</strong> A Non-Recursive query only happens when the server being asked <strong>already has the answer</strong> right there on its hard drive. There is no hunting required.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Cached Answer:</strong> You type amazon.com. Your laptop asks your Resolver. The Resolver <em>just</em> looked this up for another neighbor 5 seconds ago, so it has it memorized (cached). It hands it to you instantly.</p></li><li><p><strong>Authoritative Answer:</strong> The Resolver reaches the final Authoritative Server for amazon.com. It asks for the IP. Because this server is the official owner of amazon.com, it doesn''t need to ask anyone else. It just hands over the answer.</p></li></ol></li><li><p><strong>Real-World Analogy:</strong> You ask your friend, <em>"What is your phone number?"</em> They don''t need to go look it up in a phonebook; they just instantly tell you because they own it.</p></li></ul>

</div></li><li><p><br></p></li></div></ul></span>

', '2026-07-22 05:58:42.746', '2026-07-22 07:36:05.517');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (7, 41, 'Note 2Image Test Note', '<div style="color: white;"><img src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mNk+M9QDwADhgGAWjR9awAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==" style="border-radius: 0.375rem; margin: 0.5rem 0px;"></div><span style="color: white;">Copy Image HTML</span><span style="color: white;">function copyHtml(){try{const range = document.createRange();range.selectNode(document.getElementById(''copySource''));window.getSelection().removeAllRanges();window.getSelection().addRange(range);const success = document.execCommand(''copy'');window.getSelection().removeAllRanges();if(success){document.body.innerHTML += ''&lt;div&gt;Copy Success!&lt;/div&gt;'';}else{document.body.innerHTML += ''&lt;div&gt;Copy Failed!&lt;/div&gt;'';}}catch(err){document.body.innerHTML += ''&lt;div&gt;Copy Error: '' + err + ''&lt;/div&gt;'';}}</span>


